<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Abolition and the End of Slavery</h1><p class="ws-lede">Campaigners, writers and the enslaved themselves, and the laws that ended slavery in the Americas.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/abolition-and-the-end-of-slavery">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 5 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Olaudah Equiano</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Frederick Douglass</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Thomas Clarkson</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Saint-Domingue</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Slavery Abolition Act 1833</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Their pages on the path will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Thomas Clarkson</li><li>Olaudah Equiano</li><li>Toussaint Louverture</li><li>William Wilberforce</li><li>Frederick Douglass</li><li>Harriet Tubman</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 1822–1913</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 1745–1797</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1760–1846</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1743–1803</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 1818–1895</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1759–1833</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Atlantic slave trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Coffee economy</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Newspaper</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Slavery</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Abolitionism</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Abolitionism grew from Quaker and evangelical protest into the first great campaigns of petitions and boycotts, and between 1787 and 1888 ended legal slavery across the Americas.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>William Wilberforce</b>: &#8220;Wilberforce introduced abolition bills in Parliament from 1789 until the Slave Trade Act passed in 1807, then campaigned for the abolition of slavery itself.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Brazilian Empire</b>: &#8220;Coffee grown by enslaved people drove Brazil's wealth; Princess Isabel signed the Lei Áurea in 1888, and the planters' lost support let an army coup end the empire in 1889.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Campaigners, writers and the enslaved themselves, and the laws that ended slavery in the Americas.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
