<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Abolition and the End of Slavery</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Saint-Domingue <span>from 1665 CE</span></li><li>Olaudah Equiano <span>born c. 1745 CE</span></li><li>Thomas Clarkson <span>born 1760 CE</span></li><li>Frederick Douglass <span>born c. 1818 CE</span></li><li>Slavery Abolition Act 1833 <span>1833</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Thomas Clarkson <span>3: 1760–1846</span></li><li>Olaudah Equiano <span>2: c. 1745–1797</span></li><li>Toussaint Louverture <span>4: 1743–1803</span></li><li>William Wilberforce <span>6: 1759–1833</span></li><li>Frederick Douglass <span>5: c. 1818–1895</span></li><li>Harriet Tubman <span>1: c. 1822–1913</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Atlantic slave trade</dt><dd>The Atlantic slave trade was the forced transportation of millions of enslaved Africans to the Americas between the sixteenth and nineteenth centuries.</dd><dt>Coffee economy</dt><dd>Coffee is a drink made from the roasted seeds of the coffee plant, originally from Ethiopia and Yemen, which spread through the Islamic world and then Europe.</dd><dt>Newspaper</dt><dd>A newspaper is a periodical publication of written news about current events; the German-language Relation, printed from 1605 by Johann Carolus in Strasbourg, in the Holy Roman Empire, is often recognised as the first.</dd><dt>Slavery</dt><dd>Slavery is the condition in which people are treated as property and forced to work.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
